      {
        "id": "stage-11-topic-3-section-08",
        "title": "CRDs and the crds Directory",
        "content": "## CRDs and the crds Directory\n\n### Technical Purpose and Chart Design\nExplain how Helm handles CustomResourceDefinition files placed in crds, including installation behavior and lifecycle limitations compared with ordinary templates. In the context of Chart Structure, this area determines how chart authors organize application packaging and expose configuration to users. A well-designed chart separates reusable deployment logic from environment-specific settings. It should be understandable to another engineer, produce predictable Kubernetes resources, and make the supported configuration options clear without requiring operators to modify template internals.\n\n### File Responsibilities and Processing\nUnderstand which Helm file or directory owns this behavior and when Helm reads it. Chart metadata is interpreted during chart loading and dependency processing. Default values provide the starting configuration for template rendering. Template files use those values to generate Kubernetes manifests, while helper definitions provide reusable expressions. Supporting files and dependencies may contribute additional configuration or resources. Knowing these boundaries helps prevent placing configuration in the wrong file or expecting a file to behave like a template when it is not processed as one.\n\n### Practical Inspection\nUse the following command to inspect the relevant chart information:\n\nGet-ChildItem .\\sample-chart\\crds\n\nCompare the result with the chart's intended design. Check filenames, YAML indentation, field types, version declarations, template references, and configuration paths. When a value is consumed by a template, verify that the path and expected type match. For a chart intended for multiple environments, keep common defaults in the chart and provide deliberate overrides through separate configuration files or controlled command-line values.\n\n### Implementation and DevOps Workflow\nCreate and maintain the chart in version control. Review metadata and configuration changes alongside template changes because a seemingly small values-file modification can alter generated resource names, ports, replica counts, security settings, or storage configuration. Validate the chart before packaging it, render manifests with representative environment values, and inspect the output before allowing a CI/CD pipeline to publish or deploy the package.\n\n### Validation and Common Errors\nTypical problems include missing metadata, unsupported field values, invalid YAML, incorrect nested value paths, unexpected type conversions, missing dependency declarations, and template references to undefined values. Use Helm linting and template rendering to isolate chart-level issues before interacting with a cluster. If rendering succeeds but deployment fails, investigate the generated Kubernetes manifests and API validation separately; chart correctness does not guarantee that every rendered resource is valid for every cluster version.\n\n### Production Maintainability and Security\nUse meaningful chart versions, document configurable values, and define sensible defaults. Avoid storing passwords, tokens, private keys, or other sensitive data as ordinary chart defaults. Use secret-management integrations and restrict access to release configuration where necessary. Keep dependencies versioned and review upgrades for compatibility. A production-ready chart should be reproducible, testable, documented, and maintainable by engineers who did not originally create it."
      },
      {
        "id": "stage-11-topic-3-section-09",
        "title": "Files and ConfigMap Data",
        "content": "## Files and ConfigMap Data\n\n### Technical Purpose and Chart Design\nExplain how chart files can be included in templates using the Files object and why file size, encoding, and Kubernetes object limits matter. In the context of Chart Structure, this area determines how chart authors organize application packaging and expose configuration to users. A well-designed chart separates reusable deployment logic from environment-specific settings. It should be understandable to another engineer, produce predictable Kubernetes resources, and make the supported configuration options clear without requiring operators to modify template internals.\n\n### File Responsibilities and Processing\nUnderstand which Helm file or directory owns this behavior and when Helm reads it. Chart metadata is interpreted during chart loading and dependency processing. Default values provide the starting configuration for template rendering. Template files use those values to generate Kubernetes manifests, while helper definitions provide reusable expressions. Supporting files and dependencies may contribute additional configuration or resources. Knowing these boundaries helps prevent placing configuration in the wrong file or expecting a file to behave like a template when it is not processed as one.\n\n### Practical Inspection\nUse the following command to inspect the relevant chart information:\n\nhelm template demo ./sample-chart --debug\n\nCompare the result with the chart's intended design. Check filenames, YAML indentation, field types, version declarations, template references, and configuration paths. When a value is consumed by a template, verify that the path and expected type match. For a chart intended for multiple environments, keep common defaults in the chart and provide deliberate overrides through separate configuration files or controlled command-line values.\n\n### Implementation and DevOps Workflow\nCreate and maintain the chart in version control. Review metadata and configuration changes alongside template changes because a seemingly small values-file modification can alter generated resource names, ports, replica counts, security settings, or storage configuration. Validate the chart before packaging it, render manifests with representative environment values, and inspect the output before allowing a CI/CD pipeline to publish or deploy the package.\n\n### Validation and Common Errors\nTypical problems include missing metadata, unsupported field values, invalid YAML, incorrect nested value paths, unexpected type conversions, missing dependency declarations, and template references to undefined values. Use Helm linting and template rendering to isolate chart-level issues before interacting with a cluster. If rendering succeeds but deployment fails, investigate the generated Kubernetes manifests and API validation separately; chart correctness does not guarantee that every rendered resource is valid for every cluster version.\n\n### Production Maintainability and Security\nUse meaningful chart versions, document configurable values, and define sensible defaults. Avoid storing passwords, tokens, private keys, or other sensitive data as ordinary chart defaults. Use secret-management integrations and restrict access to release configuration where necessary. Keep dependencies versioned and review upgrades for compatibility. A production-ready chart should be reproducible, testable, documented, and maintainable by engineers who did not originally create it."
      },
      {
        "id": "stage-11-topic-3-section-10",
        "title": "Library Charts and Reusable Components",
        "content": "## Library Charts and Reusable Components\n\n### Technical Purpose and Chart Design\nExplain library chart behavior, shared template definitions, dependency reuse, and how platform teams can distribute common chart helpers. In the context of Chart Structure, this area determines how chart authors organize application packaging and expose configuration to users. A well-designed chart separates reusable deployment logic from environment-specific settings. It should be understandable to another engineer, produce predictable Kubernetes resources, and make the supported configuration options clear without requiring operators to modify template internals.\n\n### File Responsibilities and Processing\nUnderstand which Helm file or directory owns this behavior and when Helm reads it. Chart metadata is interpreted during chart loading and dependency processing. Default values provide the starting configuration for template rendering. Template files use those values to generate Kubernetes manifests, while helper definitions provide reusable expressions. Supporting files and dependencies may contribute additional configuration or resources. Knowing these boundaries helps prevent placing configuration in the wrong file or expecting a file to behave like a template when it is not processed as one.\n\n### Practical Inspection\nUse the following command to inspect the relevant chart information:\n\nhelm show chart <library-chart-reference>\n\nCompare the result with the chart's intended design. Check filenames, YAML indentation, field types, version declarations, template references, and configuration paths. When a value is consumed by a template, verify that the path and expected type match. For a chart intended for multiple environments, keep common defaults in the chart and provide deliberate overrides through separate configuration files or controlled command-line values.\n\n### Implementation and DevOps Workflow\nCreate and maintain the chart in version control. Review metadata and configuration changes alongside template changes because a seemingly small values-file modification can alter generated resource names, ports, replica counts, security settings, or storage configuration. Validate the chart before packaging it, render manifests with representative environment values, and inspect the output before allowing a CI/CD pipeline to publish or deploy the package.\n\n### Validation and Common Errors\nTypical problems include missing metadata, unsupported field values, invalid YAML, incorrect nested value paths, unexpected type conversions, missing dependency declarations, and template references to undefined values. Use Helm linting and template rendering to isolate chart-level issues before interacting with a cluster. If rendering succeeds but deployment fails, investigate the generated Kubernetes manifests and API validation separately; chart correctness does not guarantee that every rendered resource is valid for every cluster version.\n\n### Production Maintainability and Security\nUse meaningful chart versions, document configurable values, and define sensible defaults. Avoid storing passwords, tokens, private keys, or other sensitive data as ordinary chart defaults. Use secret-management integrations and restrict access to release configuration where necessary. Keep dependencies versioned and review upgrades for compatibility. A production-ready chart should be reproducible, testable, documented, and maintainable by engineers who did not originally create it."
      },
      {
        "id": "stage-11-topic-3-section-11",
        "title": "Chart Packaging and Archive Structure",
        "content": "## Chart Packaging and Archive Structure\n\n### Technical Purpose and Chart Design\nExplain how helm package creates a versioned chart archive and why package contents, metadata, and reproducibility matter for distribution. In the context of Chart Structure, this area determines how chart authors organize application packaging and expose configuration to users. A well-designed chart separates reusable deployment logic from environment-specific settings. It should be understandable to another engineer, produce predictable Kubernetes resources, and make the supported configuration options clear without requiring operators to modify template internals.\n\n### File Responsibilities and Processing\nUnderstand which Helm file or directory owns this behavior and when Helm reads it. Chart metadata is interpreted during chart loading and dependency processing. Default values provide the starting configuration for template rendering. Template files use those values to generate Kubernetes manifests, while helper definitions provide reusable expressions. Supporting files and dependencies may contribute additional configuration or resources. Knowing these boundaries helps prevent placing configuration in the wrong file or expecting a file to behave like a template when it is not processed as one.\n\n### Practical Inspection\nUse the following command to inspect the relevant chart information:\n\nhelm package ./sample-chart\n\nCompare the result with the chart's intended design. Check filenames, YAML indentation, field types, version declarations, template references, and configuration paths. When a value is consumed by a template, verify that the path and expected type match. For a chart intended for multiple environments, keep common defaults in the chart and provide deliberate overrides through separate configuration files or controlled command-line values.\n\n### Implementation and DevOps Workflow\nCreate and maintain the chart in version control. Review metadata and configuration changes alongside template changes because a seemingly small values-file modification can alter generated resource names, ports, replica counts, security settings, or storage configuration. Validate the chart before packaging it, render manifests with representative environment values, and inspect the output before allowing a CI/CD pipeline to publish or deploy the package.\n\n### Validation and Common Errors\nTypical problems include missing metadata, unsupported field values, invalid YAML, incorrect nested value paths, unexpected type conversions, missing dependency declarations, and template references to undefined values. Use Helm linting and template rendering to isolate chart-level issues before interacting with a cluster. If rendering succeeds but deployment fails, investigate the generated Kubernetes manifests and API validation separately; chart correctness does not guarantee that every rendered resource is valid for every cluster version.\n\n### Production Maintainability and Security\nUse meaningful chart versions, document configurable values, and define sensible defaults. Avoid storing passwords, tokens, private keys, or other sensitive data as ordinary chart defaults. Use secret-management integrations and restrict access to release configuration where necessary. Keep dependencies versioned and review upgrades for compatibility. A production-ready chart should be reproducible, testable, documented, and maintainable by engineers who did not originally create it."
      },
      {
        "id": "stage-11-topic-3-section-12",
        "title": "Chart Structure Validation and Maintenance",
        "content": "## Chart Structure Validation and Maintenance\n\n### Technical Purpose and Chart Design\nExplain how to validate chart structure, detect missing files and invalid templates, and organize a chart for long-term team ownership. In the context of Chart Structure, this area determines how chart authors organize application packaging and expose configuration to users. A well-designed chart separates reusable deployment logic from environment-specific settings. It should be understandable to another engineer, produce predictable Kubernetes resources, and make the supported configuration options clear without requiring operators to modify template internals.\n\n### File Responsibilities and Processing\nUnderstand which Helm file or directory owns this behavior and when Helm reads it. Chart metadata is interpreted during chart loading and dependency processing. Default values provide the starting configuration for template rendering. Template files use those values to generate Kubernetes manifests, while helper definitions provide reusable expressions. Supporting files and dependencies may contribute additional configuration or resources. Knowing these boundaries helps prevent placing configuration in the wrong file or expecting a file to behave like a template when it is not processed as one.\n\n### Practical Inspection\nUse the following command to inspect the relevant chart information:\n\nhelm lint ./sample-chart\n\nCompare the result with the chart's intended design. Check filenames, YAML indentation, field types, version declarations, template references, and configuration paths. When a value is consumed by a template, verify that the path and expected type match. For a chart intended for multiple environments, keep common defaults in the chart and provide deliberate overrides through separate configuration files or controlled command-line values.\n\n### Implementation and DevOps Workflow\nCreate and maintain the chart in version control. Review metadata and configuration changes alongside template changes because a seemingly small values-file modification can alter generated resource names, ports, replica counts, security settings, or storage configuration. Validate the chart before packaging it, render manifests with representative environment values, and inspect the output before allowing a CI/CD pipeline to publish or deploy the package.\n\n### Validation and Common Errors\nTypical problems include missing metadata, unsupported field values, invalid YAML, incorrect nested value paths, unexpected type conversions, missing dependency declarations, and template references to undefined values. Use Helm linting and template rendering to isolate chart-level issues before interacting with a cluster. If rendering succeeds but deployment fails, investigate the generated Kubernetes manifests and API validation separately; chart correctness does not guarantee that every rendered resource is valid for every cluster version.\n\n### Production Maintainability and Security\nUse meaningful chart versions, document configurable values, and define sensible defaults. Avoid storing passwords, tokens, private keys, or other sensitive data as ordinary chart defaults. Use secret-management integrations and restrict access to release configuration where necessary. Keep dependencies versioned and review upgrades for compatibility. A production-ready chart should be reproducible, testable, documented, and maintainable by engineers who did not originally create it."
      }
    ]
  },
  {
    "topicTitle": "Chart.yaml and values.yaml",
    "sections": [
      {
        "id": "stage-11-topic-4-section-01",
        "title": "Chart.yaml Required Metadata",
        "content": "## Chart.yaml Required Metadata\n\n### Technical Purpose and Chart Design\nExplain the required chart metadata fields, including apiVersion, name, and version, and how Helm uses them to identify and process a chart. In the context of Chart.yaml and values.yaml, this area determines how chart authors organize application packaging and expose configuration to users. A well-designed chart separates reusable deployment logic from environment-specific settings. It should be understandable to another engineer, produce predictable Kubernetes resources, and make the supported configuration options clear without requiring operators to modify template internals.\n\n### File Responsibilities and Processing\nUnderstand which Helm file or directory owns this behavior and when Helm reads it. Chart metadata is interpreted during chart loading and dependency processing. Default values provide the starting configuration for template rendering. Template files use those values to generate Kubernetes manifests, while helper definitions provide reusable expressions. Supporting files and dependencies may contribute additional configuration or resources. Knowing these boundaries helps prevent placing configuration in the wrong file or expecting a file to behave like a template when it is not processed as one.\n\n### Practical Inspection\nUse the following command to inspect the relevant chart information:\n\nGet-Content .\\sample-chart\\Chart.yaml\n\nCompare the result with the chart's intended design. Check filenames, YAML indentation, field types, version declarations, template references, and configuration paths. When a value is consumed by a template, verify that the path and expected type match. For a chart intended for multiple environments, keep common defaults in the chart and provide deliberate overrides through separate configuration files or controlled command-line values.\n\n### Implementation and DevOps Workflow\nCreate and maintain the chart in version control. Review metadata and configuration changes alongside template changes because a seemingly small values-file modification can alter generated resource names, ports, replica counts, security settings, or storage configuration. Validate the chart before packaging it, render manifests with representative environment values, and inspect the output before allowing a CI/CD pipeline to publish or deploy the package.\n\n### Validation and Common Errors\nTypical problems include missing metadata, unsupported field values, invalid YAML, incorrect nested value paths, unexpected type conversions, missing dependency declarations, and template references to undefined values. Use Helm linting and template rendering to isolate chart-level issues before interacting with a cluster. If rendering succeeds but deployment fails, investigate the generated Kubernetes manifests and API validation separately; chart correctness does not guarantee that every rendered resource is valid for every cluster version.\n\n### Production Maintainability and Security\nUse meaningful chart versions, document configurable values, and define sensible defaults. Avoid storing passwords, tokens, private keys, or other sensitive data as ordinary chart defaults. Use secret-management integrations and restrict access to release configuration where necessary. Keep dependencies versioned and review upgrades for compatibility. A production-ready chart should be reproducible, testable, documented, and maintainable by engineers who did not originally create it."
      },
